# ============================================================
# COMPONENT RULES
# ============================================================

## General
- One component per file
- File name matches component name exactly (PascalCase)
- Always use named exports for components, not default exports
  Exception: page components may use default export if needed
- Always type all props with a TypeScript interface above 
  the component
- Never use `any` unless it already exists in the file
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Keep components under 300 lines — split if larger
- Extract repeated JSX blocks into sub-components

## Props interface pattern
  interface ComponentNameProps {
    value: string
    onChange: (value: string) => void
    disabled?: boolean
    className?: string
  }

## Component file structure (in this order)
  1. Imports (external libs first, then internal)
  2. Types/interfaces
  3. Constants (outside component)
  4. Component function
  5. Sub-components (if small and tightly coupled)
  6. Export

## Styling
- Use Tailwind utility classes only
- Never write inline styles except for dynamic values
  (e.g. style={{ width: `${progress}%` }})
- Never write CSS modules or styled-components
- Use cn() from src/lib/utils for conditional classes:
  className={cn('base-class', condition && 'conditional-class')}
- Use the existing components/ui/ primitives before 
  building new ones
- Follow existing color patterns in the app — check 
  neighboring components for the right tokens

## Icons
- Use lucide-react exclusively for icons
- Import only what you need: import { X, Plus } from 'lucide-react'
- Standard sizes: size={16} for inline, size={20} for buttons,
  size={24} for headers

## Accessibility
- All interactive elements must have accessible labels
- Images must have alt text
- Form inputs must have associated labels
- Use semantic HTML: nav, main, section, article, header, footer
- Buttons must have descriptive text or aria-label
- Focus states must be visible (do not remove outline)

# ============================================================
# CREATING NEW PAGES
# ============================================================

## File location
- Member app pages: src/app/pages/app/PageName.tsx
- Admin pages: src/app/pages/admin/PageName.tsx
- Onboarding: src/app/pages/onboarding/PageName.tsx
- Auth pages: src/app/pages/auth/PageName.tsx

## After creating a page you MUST also
1. Add a lazy import in App.tsx:
   const PageName = lazy(() => import('@/app/pages/app/PageName'))
2. Add the route inside the correct Suspense boundary in App.tsx
3. Add a nav link in AppLayout.tsx or AdminLayout.tsx if needed

## Page template — member app page
  import { useState } from 'react'
  import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query'
  import { api } from '@/lib/api'
  import { queryKeys } from '@/lib/queries'
  import { toast } from 'sonner'

  export function PageName() {
    const { data, isLoading, isError } = useQuery({
      queryKey: queryKeys.something(),
      queryFn: () => api.something.getAll(),
      staleTime: 60_000,
    })

    if (isLoading) return <PageSkeleton />
    if (isError) return <ErrorState />

    return (
      <div className="space-y-6 p-6">
        <div className="flex items-center justify-between">
          <h1 className="text-2xl font-bold">Page Title</h1>
        </div>
        {/* page content */}
      </div>
    )
  }

## Page template — admin page
  import { useState } from 'react'
  import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query'
  import { api } from '@/lib/api'
  import { toast } from 'sonner'

  export function AdminPageName() {
    const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({
      queryKey: ['admin', 'something'],
      queryFn: () => api.admin.getSomething(),
      staleTime: 60_000,
    })

    return (
      <div className="space-y-6">
        <div className="flex items-center justify-between">
          <div>
            <h1 className="text-2xl font-bold">Admin Page</h1>
            <p className="text-muted-foreground text-sm">
              Description of this admin section
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        {/* admin content */}
      </div>
    )
  }

## Loading states
- Always show a skeleton while data loads
- Use existing AdminTableSkeleton for admin tables
- Use card skeletons for dashboard widgets
- Never show empty UI while loading

## Error states
- Always handle isError from useQuery
- Show a user-friendly message with a retry button
- Use toast.error() for mutation failures
- Never expose raw error messages to users

# ============================================================
# CREATING NEW COMPONENTS (UI primitives)
# ============================================================

Location: src/components/ui/ComponentName.tsx

- Must use Radix UI primitives as base where applicable
- Must be fully accessible (keyboard nav, screen readers)
- Must accept className prop for Tailwind overrides
- Must use cn() for class merging
- Must be unstyled/themeable — no hardcoded colors
- Export the component and its props interface

Example pattern:
  import * as RadixDialog from '@radix-ui/react-dialog'
  import { cn } from '@/lib/utils'

  interface DialogProps {
    open: boolean
    onOpenChange: (open: boolean) => void
    children: React.ReactNode
    className?: string
  }

  export function Dialog({ open, onOpenChange, children, className }: DialogProps) {
    return (
      <RadixDialog.Root open={open} onOpenChange={onOpenChange}>
        <RadixDialog.Portal>
          <RadixDialog.Overlay className="fixed inset-0 bg-black/50" />
          <RadixDialog.Content className={cn('...', className)}>
            {children}
          </RadixDialog.Content>
        </RadixDialog.Portal>
      </RadixDialog.Root>
    )
  }

# ============================================================
# DASHBOARD WIDGETS
# ============================================================

- Each dashboard widget is its own component in the page file
  or in a widgets/ subfolder if reused
- Wrap in React.memo() if it receives stable props
- Use recharts for all charts (already installed)
- Chart wrapper pattern using components/ui/chart.tsx:
  import { ChartContainer, ChartTooltip } from '@/components/ui/chart'
- Stats cards use the existing StatsCard component
- All dashboard data comes from useQuery — never fetch in widgets
- Pass data down as props to widget components
- Widget components should be pure (no internal fetching)

Dashboard layout pattern:
  <div className="grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
    <StatsCard title="Total Users" value={stats.users} />
    <StatsCard title="Active Sessions" value={stats.sessions} />
  </div>
  <div className="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <ChartWidget data={chartData} />
    <TableWidget data={tableData} />
  </div>

# ============================================================
# FORMS
# ============================================================

- Use react-hook-form + zod for ALL forms
- Define zod schema above the component
- Use useForm with zodResolver
- Never use uncontrolled inputs without react-hook-form
- Always show validation errors inline below the input
- Disable submit button while submitting
- Use useMutation for form submissions with optimistic updates
  where appropriate

Form pattern:
  import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form'
  import { zodResolver } from '@hookform/resolvers/zod'
  import { z } from 'zod'

  const schema = z.object({
    name: z.string().min(1, 'Name is required'),
    email: z.string().email('Invalid email'),
  })

  type FormData = z.infer<typeof schema>

  export function MyForm() {
    const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors, isSubmitting } } = useForm<FormData>({
      resolver: zodResolver(schema),
    })

    const mutation = useMutation({
      mutationFn: (data: FormData) => api.something.create(data),
      onSuccess: () => toast.success('Saved'),
      onError: () => toast.error('Failed to save'),
    })

    return (
      <form onSubmit={handleSubmit((data) => mutation.mutate(data))}>
        <input {...register('name')} />
        {errors.name && <p className="text-red-500 text-sm">{errors.name.message}</p>}
        <button type="submit" disabled={isSubmitting || mutation.isPending}>
          Save
        </button>
      </form>
    )
  }

# ============================================================
# TABLES (admin pages)
# ============================================================

- Use the existing AdminTableSkeleton for loading state
- Use AdminPaginationBar for pagination
- All admin tables must have:
  - Column headers
  - Loading skeleton
  - Empty state message
  - Pagination (never load all rows at once)
  - Row actions (edit, delete, view) in the last column
- Search and filter state goes in useState (not URL params 
  unless specifically requested)
- Default page size: 20 rows
- Use useMutation for row actions with optimistic updates

# ============================================================
# MODALS AND DIALOGS
# ============================================================

- Use Radix Dialog via components/ui/dialog.tsx
- Modal state (open/close) always lives in the parent component
- Never fetch data inside a modal — pass data as props
- Always include a close button and handle Escape key 
  (Radix handles this automatically)
- Confirm destructive actions with a confirmation dialog
  before executing
- Use the existing ConfirmationModal component for deletes

# ============================================================
# TANSTACK QUERY RULES
# ============================================================

QueryClient config in main.tsx:
  staleTime: 60_000
  gcTime: 5 * 60_000
  retry: 1
  refetchOnWindowFocus: false

staleTime per data type:
  User profile:        5 * 60_000
  Emergency contacts:  5 * 60_000
  Notifications list:  30_000
  Unread count:        10_000
  Everything else:     60_000

Migration pattern — replace useEffect+useState with useQuery:
  BEFORE:
    const [data, setData] = useState(null)
    const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(true)
    useEffect(() => {
      api.something.getAll()
        .then(setData)
        .catch(() => toast.error('Failed'))
        .finally(() => setIsLoading(false))
    }, [])

  AFTER:
    const { data, isLoading } = useQuery({
      queryKey: queryKeys.something(),
      queryFn: () => api.something.getAll(),
      staleTime: 60_000,
    })

Migration rules:
  1. KEEP all UI state (modals, tabs, filters, selections)
  2. KEEP all toast/error notification logic
  3. KEEP loading skeleton UI — use isLoading from useQuery
  4. REPLACE useState+useEffect data fetching with useQuery
  5. REPLACE manual reload() calls with queryClient.invalidateQueries
  6. NEVER change component name, export, JSX layout, or styling
  7. Import queryKeys from @/lib/queries
  8. Always show full updated file after migration

Pages to migrate (in priority order):
  1.  src/app/pages/app/Dashboard.tsx
  2.  src/app/components/AppLayout.tsx
  3.  src/app/contexts/NotificationsContext.tsx
  4.  src/app/pages/app/MoodCheckIn.tsx
  5.  src/app/pages/app/Journal.tsx
  6.  src/app/pages/app/SessionHistory.tsx
  7.  src/app/pages/app/HabitTracker.tsx
  8.  src/app/pages/app/SleepTracker.tsx
  9.  src/app/pages/app/MoodHistory.tsx
  10. src/app/pages/app/Community.tsx

# ============================================================
# STATE MANAGEMENT RULES
# ============================================================

Use the right tool for the right state:

- Server data (API responses) → TanStack Query (useQuery)
- Form state → react-hook-form
- UI state (modals, tabs, toggles) → useState
- Auth state → AuthContext (already set up)
- Notifications → NotificationsContext (already set up)
- Onboarding → OnboardingContext (already set up)
- Cross-component UI state → lift to nearest common parent
- Global UI state (theme, sidebar) → Context if needed

Never use:
- Redux (not installed)
- Zustand (not installed)
- MobX (not installed)
- localStorage for server data — use TanStack Query cache

